Fixes to beautiful-mermaid 1.1.3, applied by build-vendor.sh before bundling
(patch -p2 from the unpacked package). Each is valid Mermaid the library read
or drew differently from Mermaid 11, found by diffing its parse of diagrams
Claude wrote, and of every form of each kind, against Mermaid's own parser:

  flowchart  `A-->B` with no spaces; ids with `.`, or letters outside ASCII;
             a label given after the node was first named (`A --> B`, then
             `B[Label]`); a quoted label holding the closing delimiter
             (`B("a (b)")`); parallelograms (`[/a/]`, `[\a\]`), drawn as the
             nearest trapezoid; Mermaid 11's `A@{ shape: diam, label: "x" }`;
             `-. text .-` and labels without spaces (`-.x.->`); `--o`, `--x`,
             `o--o`, `x--x` and longer links (`--->`); `~~~`, which places
             its nodes side by side and draws nothing; a space before `|label|`; `;`
             between statements; a header with no direction; `click` lines;
             a node in the first subgraph that names it, as Mermaid places it.
             Mermaid 11's edge ids (`A e1@--> B`), and their `e1@{ }` lines.
  sequence   participant names with spaces; `create participant`; two-way
             arrows (`<<->>`, `<<-->>`) with a head at each end; `option`
             inside `critical`; a message with empty text (`A->>B:`).
  state      one start and one end state in each scope; ids with `.`; a
             `:::class` after a state in a transition.
  class      `A -- B` and `A .. B`: lines with no marker.
  erDiagram  `}o` on the left of a relationship; an entity block on one line;
             keys written `PK, FK`; an alias on an entity's block
             (`A["Account"] {`), shown as its name; cardinalities and lines
             in words (`A one or more to zero or many B : has`).
  labels     entity codes (`#59;`, `#quot;`) shown as their characters.

And in the ASCII drawing, each a defect found by checking every drawing of
those diagrams for line ends that meet nothing and labels that don't survive:

  boxes      a box's sides sit where its edges meet it, so an edge never
             starts or ends a cell or two off its box when a label widened
             the column beside it.
  labels     edge labels placed one by one, each moved the least distance
             that keeps it off the labels before it and off every box (two
             labels on one line used to interleave letter by letter); the
             gaps between a label's words hide the line under them; class
             relationship labels likewise, drawn after every line.
  bundles    edges joined into one trunk only when their far ends sit side by
             side, so an edge from another row no longer runs along the
             trunk and reads as one of its edges.
  sequence   a self-message's label one line per row (a wrapped one broke the
             row), with room for it before the next lifeline.

The library's own tests of the behaviours this changes are updated to match,
golden drawings included, each reviewed against the drawing it replaces.

